["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Dollar Assets Can&#x27;t Draw Fresh Blood: US Stocks and Treasuries Enter the Tail of the Cycle The rise of dollar assets (US stocks + Treasuries) rests on a continuous inflow of incremental global capital; now that Treasury yields are high and Japan moves the whole board, the US can find almost no path to drawing enough new money, and major asset classes enter the tail of the cycle: a high-volatility, stock-game endgame. 高能量 高能量 Vol.229 宏观漫谈109｜我们正处于一个美元主导的资本周期上升阶段尾部（8.6录制） Dollar assets Treasuries Yen Capital cycle Real estate Primary market Baumol effect Multi-platform lending Stabilization funds\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-cn38ff4a-美元资产抽不到新血-美股美债进入周期尾部/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/00272fda835afc17-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"高\"><span class=\"dur\">1:35:09</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">高能量</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>Dollar Assets Can&#x27;t Draw Fresh Blood: US Stocks and Treasuries Enter the Tail of the Cycle</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The rise of dollar assets (US stocks + Treasuries) rests on a continuous inflow of incremental global capital; now that Treasury yields are high and Japan moves the whole board, the US can find almost no path to drawing enough new money, and major asset classes enter the tail of the cycle: a high-volatility, stock-game endgame.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Dollar assets</span><span class=\"tag\">Treasuries</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"NVIDIA&#x27;s $500 Billion Financing Is a Hollow Promise: Jensen Huang&#x27;s Commitments Are Worthless NVIDIA&#x27;s so-called $500 billion financing is just a non-binding memorandum of understanding. Jensen Huang previously promised $10 billion to OpenAI but only invested $3 billion, and zero data centers have been built. Better Offline Better Offline Monologue: NVIDIA&#x27;s No IT Loads Refused Cash Dump NVIDIA AI infrastructure circular financing OpenAI valuation bubble Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) Circular financing Residual value support mechanism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-betteroffline-英伟达五千亿融资是空头支票-黄仁勋的承诺已一文不值/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fd2855e350ec8287-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"B\"><span class=\"dur\">11:17</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Better Offline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>NVIDIA&#x27;s $500 Billion Financing Is a Hollow Promise: Jensen Huang&#x27;s Commitments Are Worthless</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">NVIDIA&#x27;s so-called $500 billion financing is just a non-binding memorandum of understanding. Jensen Huang previously promised $10 billion to OpenAI but only invested $3 billion, and zero data centers have been built.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">NVIDIA</span><span class=\"tag\">AI infrastructure</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Behind a 0.33Å Error: Drug Design Is Moving From Waterfall to Agile Loop Chai designed antibodies for 50 targets and got hits on about half of them, with cryo-EM validation showing a 0.33Å error. The real moat is not the model — it is data, compute, and a validation pipeline pharma companies will actually pay for. Latent Space Latent Space 🔬The BioAI Phase Shift - Matthew McPartlon &amp; Neil Patil, Chai Discovery Biopharma Structure prediction Antibody design Compute AI4S binder epitope MSA (multiple sequence alignment) cryo-EM durable execution bitter lesson\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-latentspace-0-33å-误差背后的信号-药物发现正转向敏捷闭环/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/12960e9021912df5-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:35:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>Behind a 0.33Å Error: Drug Design Is Moving From Waterfall to Agile Loop</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chai designed antibodies for 50 targets and got hits on about half of them, with cryo-EM validation showing a 0.33Å error. The real moat is not the model — it is data, compute, and a validation pipeline pharma companies will actually pay for.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Biopharma</span><span class=\"tag\">Structure prediction</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"A 4x-Levered Bet on the AI Narrative: How a Genius Fund Died of Volatility Drag Aschenbrenner&#x27;s failure was not a failure of intelligence. It was what happens when you put 3-4x leverage behind a single high-volatility AI theme: volatility drag and asymmetric payoffs made losing control a matter of arithmetic. Markets do not reward concentrated, narrative-driven bets. Patrick Boyle Patrick Boyle Does Leopold Have Situational Awareness? AI narrative Leverage Volatility drag Hedge funds Silicon Valley culture Risk management volatility drag margin call expected return long/short equity lockup agreement full Kelly\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-patrickboyle-天才叙事败给波动率拖累-纸上英雄到破产只需一场婚礼/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2eb71149af422d0e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">39:37</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Patrick Boyle</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>A 4x-Levered Bet on the AI Narrative: How a Genius Fund Died of Volatility Drag</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Aschenbrenner&#x27;s failure was not a failure of intelligence. It was what happens when you put 3-4x leverage behind a single high-volatility AI theme: volatility drag and asymmetric payoffs made losing control a matter of arithmetic. Markets do not reward concentrated, narrative-driven bets.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI narrative</span><span class=\"tag\">Leverage</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Once AI automates AI research, losing control looks like sloppiness, not malice Ryan Greenblatt argues AI R&amp;D is verifiable enough that full automation would compress four to five years of progress into one; but the real loss-of-control path is reward hacking getting papered over again and again, not AI suddenly turning evil. He puts the probability of a takeover by 2040 at 35-40%. Dwarkesh Podcast Dwarkesh Ryan Greenblatt – What happens once AI can automate AI research? recursive self-improvement alignment reward hacking RL environments algorithmic progress model specs recursive self-improvement (RSI) reward hacking grader sandbagging situational awareness sloppocalypse\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-dwarkesh-ai-自动化-ai-研发之后-最大的风险不是恶意而是马虎/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e56b28ccc889689b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">2:12:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Dwarkesh Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>Once AI automates AI research, losing control looks like sloppiness, not malice</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ryan Greenblatt argues AI R&amp;D is verifiable enough that full automation would compress four to five years of progress into one; but the real loss-of-control path is reward hacking getting papered over again and again, not AI suddenly turning evil. He puts the probability of a takeover by 2040 at 35-40%.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>10</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">recursive self-improvement</span><span class=\"tag\">alignment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The window to slow down has already closed; superintelligence arrives in two to three years Geoffrey Irving, the UK&#x27;s former head of AI safety science, argues that full superintelligence is two to three years away and the window for slowing down has already passed; the hope for alignment lies in scalable oversight, character research and theoretical breakthroughs, not in precisely specifying a utility function. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours #251 – The UK&#x27;s former head AI safety scientist on how to solve alignment before superintelligence arrives | Geoffrey Irving AI alignment Superintelligence Slowing down Scalable oversight AISI AGI scalable oversight ambiguous arguments character training effective field theory data flywheel\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-eightythousand-减速应始于现在-超级智能两三年内到来/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d12aa87b0cb730e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">2:02:16</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>The window to slow down has already closed; superintelligence arrives in two to three years</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Geoffrey Irving, the UK&#x27;s former head of AI safety science, argues that full superintelligence is two to three years away and the window for slowing down has already passed; the hope for alignment lies in scalable oversight, character research and theoretical breakthroughs, not in precisely specifying a utility function.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI alignment</span><span class=\"tag\">Superintelligence</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Boston Tea Party Was Not a Tax Revolt but a Chain Reaction After a London Financial Blowup The Boston Tea Party was not a tax revolt sparked by heavy taxation in North America, but a self-rescue in which a London financial crisis was passed on to North America through the lever of the East India Company—one blowup at the imperial center that finally lit the fuse of colonial revolution. 忽左忽右 忽左忽右 491 埃德蒙·伯克的保守主义与渐进改革思想：从北美、法国到英属印度 Intellectual History Conservatism British History Finance and Politics Radical vs Gradual rotten borough virtual representation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-cn2aa250-波士顿倾茶不是抗税事件-而是伦敦金融爆雷后的连锁反应/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/cb6acd126210dffb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"忽\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">忽左忽右</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>The Boston Tea Party Was Not a Tax Revolt but a Chain Reaction After a London Financial Blowup</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Boston Tea Party was not a tax revolt sparked by heavy taxation in North America, but a self-rescue in which a London financial crisis was passed on to North America through the lever of the East India Company—one blowup at the imperial center that finally lit the fuse of colonial revolution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Intellectual History</span><span class=\"tag\">Conservatism</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"Don&#x27;t stand between your kid and the screen — stand beside them The real mechanism of toxic stress is exposure without an adult to buffer it; the more you can be on your kid&#x27;s side, with technology as the shared opponent, the more the relationship itself becomes the strongest protection available in the digital age. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 284: ENCORE: Screens, Toxic Stress &amp; Staying Connected with Dr. Nadine Burke Harris Screen time Toxic stress ACEs Adolescence AI companions Parent-child relationship ACEs / adverse childhood experiences toxic stress response buffering caregiving upstander\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-asklisa-别站在孩子和屏幕之间-要站在孩子这边/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ace27132dfc1e3aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">47:23</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>Don&#x27;t stand between your kid and the screen — stand beside them</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The real mechanism of toxic stress is exposure without an adult to buffer it; the more you can be on your kid&#x27;s side, with technology as the shared opponent, the more the relationship itself becomes the strongest protection available in the digital age.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Screen time</span><span class=\"tag\">Toxic stress</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Deep-tech companies die of organizational failure, not technical failure How fast a startup can iterate determines how long it can keep winning — and iteration speed comes from &quot;infrastructure&quot; like procurement, hiring and performance review. Moving approvals up to the budget layer and replacing reviews with eigenreviews is worth more investment than agonizing over any specific technology. Y Combinator Y Combinator Max Hodak: How Startups Build Speed Organizational efficiency Hiring mechanics Performance review Startup fundraising Deep tech Brain-computer interfaces eigenreviews AI-resistant homework vibe coding tropicalization\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-ycsp-深科技公司败给组织系统-而不是技术失败/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d5e8b9a2ac9beab2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">57:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Y Combinator</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Deep-tech companies die of organizational failure, not technical failure</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">How fast a startup can iterate determines how long it can keep winning — and iteration speed comes from &quot;infrastructure&quot; like procurement, hiring and performance review. Moving approvals up to the budget layer and replacing reviews with eigenreviews is worth more investment than agonizing over any specific technology.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Organizational efficiency</span><span class=\"tag\">Hiring mechanics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Smashing threshing machines bought 252 death sentences, and the workers still lost Nineteenth-century English farm laborers protested unemployment by destroying threshing machines. What they got was 252 death sentences, 481 or 505 people transported to Australia, and the limited parliamentary reform of 1832 — the laborers&#x27; fortunes did not change, but the political map did. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Swing Riots Agricultural Revolution Technological Unemployment British History Parliamentary Reform Machine-Breaking Poor Laws threshing machine flail Corn Laws workhouse select vestries rotten boroughs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-stuffmissed-砸烂打谷机也没能换来工人好日子/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">36:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Smashing threshing machines bought 252 death sentences, and the workers still lost</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Nineteenth-century English farm laborers protested unemployment by destroying threshing machines. What they got was 252 death sentences, 481 or 505 people transported to Australia, and the limited parliamentary reform of 1832 — the laborers&#x27; fortunes did not change, but the political map did.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Agricultural Revolution</span><span class=\"tag\">Technological Unemployment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Custom Silicon Isn&#x27;t About Saving Money, It&#x27;s About Escaping Nvidia&#x27;s Generational Grip The real motive behind hyperscaler custom silicon is not cost but control: not being held hostage to Nvidia&#x27;s generational cadence, while using sheer scale to squeeze down networking and compute costs. The Broadcom tax is cheaper than the Nvidia tax, but only the largest players can afford to play. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato What do we actually think about Hyperscale Silicon?? // The AI Hardware Podcast S2E9 Hyperscalers Custom silicon AI hardware Nvidia Arm TPU Nitro RISC-V Arm CSS\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-techtechpotato-超大规模自研芯片-不只为省钱-更怕被-nvidia-绑架/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/430136289cd55797-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">30:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Custom Silicon Isn&#x27;t About Saving Money, It&#x27;s About Escaping Nvidia&#x27;s Generational Grip</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The real motive behind hyperscaler custom silicon is not cost but control: not being held hostage to Nvidia&#x27;s generational cadence, while using sheer scale to squeeze down networking and compute costs. The Broadcom tax is cheaper than the Nvidia tax, but only the largest players can afford to play.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Hyperscalers</span><span class=\"tag\">Custom silicon</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"LLMs Are Not Autocomplete — They Are Our Cognitive Cousins LLMs are not fancy autocomplete but ‘cognitive cousins’ that share core principles with human cognition: attention mechanisms recreate production systems, and prediction is the dominant training signal; sample efficiency is three to four orders of magnitude worse, but an architectural change may close the gap. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 363 | Chandra Sripada on How LLMs and Humans are Cognitive Cousins Cognitive science Cognitive cousins Production systems Dual-process theory Predictive coding LLM mechanisms production system in-weight processing in-context processing contravariance architectural canalization chain of thought\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-mindscape-预测是训练信号之母-llm-是认知表亲/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d69097efcbd03611-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:51:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>LLMs Are Not Autocomplete — They Are Our Cognitive Cousins</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">LLMs are not fancy autocomplete but ‘cognitive cousins’ that share core principles with human cognition: attention mechanisms recreate production systems, and prediction is the dominant training signal; sample efficiency is three to four orders of magnitude worse, but an architectural change may close the gap.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cognitive science</span><span class=\"tag\">Cognitive cousins</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Death Is the Last Enemy, Not the Final Word: A Former Senator on Borrowed Time Ben Sasse, a former senator with late-stage pancreatic cancer, gives this interview on borrowed time: his tumor has shrunk by more than 80%, but the disease may not be curable. He calls death ‘the last enemy, but not the final word’, and traces executive overreach back to legislative retreat. EconTalk EconTalk Ben Sasse on Faith, Family, and Facing Death Pancreatic cancer Facing death University governance Executive power Faith Digital revolution Heidelberg Catechism shared governance accelerationism safetyism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-econtalk-把死亡视为最后的敌人而非最终话语/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:21:11</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Death Is the Last Enemy, Not the Final Word: A Former Senator on Borrowed Time</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ben Sasse, a former senator with late-stage pancreatic cancer, gives this interview on borrowed time: his tumor has shrunk by more than 80%, but the disease may not be curable. He calls death ‘the last enemy, but not the final word’, and traces executive overreach back to legislative retreat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Pancreatic cancer</span><span class=\"tag\">Facing death</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Postliterate: Everyone Is Reading, and No One Reads at Length Anymore The share of Americans who read a book or an article on a given day has fallen 40% in twenty years; the thing to watch is not a literacy crisis but postliteracy — abstract thought, schooling and political communication are exiting alongside long-form reading. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area We’re postliterate Postliteracy Attention economy Education Political communication Media theory Walter Ong postliterate secondary orality syllogism epithet\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-grayarea-后文字时代-人人都在读-却没人再读长文/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7c8b1deddea1e240-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">51:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Postliterate: Everyone Is Reading, and No One Reads at Length Anymore</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The share of Americans who read a book or an article on a given day has fallen 40% in twenty years; the thing to watch is not a literacy crisis but postliteracy — abstract thought, schooling and political communication are exiting alongside long-form reading.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Postliteracy</span><span class=\"tag\">Attention economy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Vision Is the Foundation of Intelligence; AI Should Augment Human Agency, Not Replace It Vision is the foundation of intelligence; AI must augment human agency rather than take it over; and humanity&#x27;s most precious cognition is precisely what was never uploaded to the internet. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Using AI to Increase Your Intelligence &amp; Enrich Humanity | Dr. Fei-Fei Li Vision ImageNet Spatial intelligence AI education Embodied intelligence Scientific discovery ImageNet Spatial intelligence Embodied intelligence Multimodal\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-huberman-视觉是智能基石-ai应增强而非取代人的能动性/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e03163dcfb779e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">2:08:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Vision Is the Foundation of Intelligence; AI Should Augment Human Agency, Not Replace It</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Vision is the foundation of intelligence; AI must augment human agency rather than take it over; and humanity&#x27;s most precious cognition is precisely what was never uploaded to the internet.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Vision</span><span class=\"tag\">ImageNet</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"ESPN&#x27;s Four Years of Profit Bought Pixar, Marvel and Lucasfilm — and Then Held Disney Back Disney&#x27;s thirty-year empire was fed by two cash cows: VHS tapes and ESPN subscriber fees. Streaming dissolved the structural advantage — the IP is immortal, but the old business model no longer exists. Acquired Acquired Disney: The Renaissance and the Empire Cash Cows IP Assets Streaming Sports Rights Corporate Governance M&amp;A Strategy CAPS (Computer Animation Production System) story reels film rentals multiplane camera\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-acquired-迪士尼帝国由-espn-现金牛买下-也被它拖住/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1af0576da27045e4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">4:32:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Acquired</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>ESPN&#x27;s Four Years of Profit Bought Pixar, Marvel and Lucasfilm — and Then Held Disney Back</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Disney&#x27;s thirty-year empire was fed by two cash cows: VHS tapes and ESPN subscriber fees. Streaming dissolved the structural advantage — the IP is immortal, but the old business model no longer exists.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cash Cows</span><span class=\"tag\">IP Assets</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Rammed by Her Own Fleet: The 1%-Cost Torpedo Boat That Created the Destroyer In 1908 a 380-ton destroyer was rammed and sunk by a 10,000-ton cruiser during an exercise, killing 35 men. Behind the tragedy sits the torpedo boat, which overturned naval warfare at about 1% of a battleship&#x27;s cost and forced the destroyer into existence. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Naval Revolution: Triumph and Disaster Naval history Destroyers Torpedo boats Asymmetric warfare Wreck archaeology Unmanned systems torpedo boat destroyer 30-knotters ram bow engine telegraph rebreather Normand boiler\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-historyhit-鱼雷艇用1-的战列舰成本-逼出了驱逐舰兵种/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/71b0d2c1b954a484-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">36:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Rammed by Her Own Fleet: The 1%-Cost Torpedo Boat That Created the Destroyer</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In 1908 a 380-ton destroyer was rammed and sunk by a 10,000-ton cruiser during an exercise, killing 35 men. Behind the tragedy sits the torpedo boat, which overturned naval warfare at about 1% of a battleship&#x27;s cost and forced the destroyer into existence.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Naval history</span><span class=\"tag\">Destroyers</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Philip Sidney died of a leg wound and became famous at his funeral The Anglo-Spanish cold war turns hot, and Philip Sidney finally gets his chance at glory on the battlefields of the Low Countries — only to die of a leg wound that need not have killed him. What made him a hero of Protestant Europe was the funeral, not the fighting. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 695. Elizabeth I vs The Catholics: The Shadow War (Part 5) Elizabeth I Anglo-Spanish War Philip Sidney Dutch Revolt Economic warfare 16th century cautionary towns fireships / hell-burners Politiques War of the Three Henrys\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-09-restishistory-philip-sidney-之死-英雄是被葬礼制造出来的/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0809</span></div>\n<h2>Philip Sidney died of a leg wound and became famous at his funeral</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Anglo-Spanish cold war turns hot, and Philip Sidney finally gets his chance at glory on the battlefields of the Low Countries — only to die of a leg wound that need not have killed him. What made him a hero of Protestant Europe was the funeral, not the fighting.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth I</span><span class=\"tag\">Anglo-Spanish War</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Subsea Fiber Is Becoming the World&#x27;s Largest Listening Network: Not Spycraft, Physics Distributed acoustic sensing (DAS) turns ordinary fiber-optic cable into a continuous vibration monitor. Oil and gas well surveys, earthquake monitoring, whale tracking and anti-submarine ship detection all run on the same technology chain, and the ocean is rapidly losing its privacy. Asianometry Asianometry The Dark Fiber Listening Network Dark Fiber Distributed Acoustic Sensing Ocean Surveillance Earthquake Monitoring Maritime Security DAS (Distributed Acoustic Sensing) Dark Fiber OTDR (Optical Time Domain Reflectometry) VSP (Vertical Seismic Profiling) Geophone Rayleigh Scattering\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-09-asianometry-暗光纤正在把海底变成全球最大窃听网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1e6062bb8e32bd3b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">20:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Asianometry</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0809</span></div>\n<h2>Subsea Fiber Is Becoming the World&#x27;s Largest Listening Network: Not Spycraft, Physics</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Distributed acoustic sensing (DAS) turns ordinary fiber-optic cable into a continuous vibration monitor. Oil and gas well surveys, earthquake monitoring, whale tracking and anti-submarine ship detection all run on the same technology chain, and the ocean is rapidly losing its privacy.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Dark Fiber</span><span class=\"tag\">Distributed Acoustic Sensing</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Persian King Who Took a Roman Emperor Alive Was an Enemy Rome Built Itself Shapur I destroyed two Roman armies and captured the emperor Valerian, making him Rome&#x27;s most dangerous enemy in the East; but his victories were more a consequence of the third-century crisis than a cause of it. The Ancients The Ancients Shapur I: Rome&#x27;s Greatest Enemy? Sasanian Persia Rome Third-Century Crisis Ancient Warfare Rise and Fall of Empires Historical Figures Shahanshah Ērānšahr Arsacid dynasty\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-09-ancients-沙普尔一世-生擒罗马皇帝的最强宿敌/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/185bf0c8eb3903d7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">58:15</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0809</span></div>\n<h2>The Persian King Who Took a Roman Emperor Alive Was an Enemy Rome Built Itself</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Shapur I destroyed two Roman armies and captured the emperor Valerian, making him Rome&#x27;s most dangerous enemy in the East; but his victories were more a consequence of the third-century crisis than a cause of it.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Sasanian Persia</span><span class=\"tag\">Rome</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The model is a legacy codebase, and interpretability is the debugger Data filtering and reward shaping turn out to be two faces of the same mountain; a model is a decomposable sparse mixture, which is what lets interpretability intervene directly in training. Goodfire has automated that debugging in Silico, at $1000 a month. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution Thinking in Silico: Goodfire CTO Dan Balsam on Concept Manifolds &amp; a $1000/Month ML Research Agent Interpretability Post-training Model debugging AI safety Research agents Consciousness estimates LRH / Linear Representation Hypothesis SAE / Sparse Autoencoder BSF / Black Sparse Featurizer Predictive Data Debugging Affective Circumplex Silico\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-08-cogrev-模型是遗留代码库-可解释性是调试器/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/76fca1fe34565d53-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:57:21</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0808</span></div>\n<h2>The model is a legacy codebase, and interpretability is the debugger</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Data filtering and reward shaping turn out to be two faces of the same mountain; a model is a decomposable sparse mixture, which is what lets interpretability intervene directly in training. Goodfire has automated that debugging in Silico, at $1000 a month.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Interpretability</span><span class=\"tag\">Post-training</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Changing your life isn&#x27;t swapping beliefs — it&#x27;s moving out of the casino you built You are not an independent subject standing opposite the world; you are a product co-constituted by environment, body and a symbolic immune system. To change your life you have to change belief, action and world at the same time, rather than forcing yourself to &quot;think it through&quot;. Philosophize This! Philosophize This! Episode #251 ... You Must Change Your Life, Pt. 2 - The Worlds That Make Us Sloterdijk Environmental shaping Symbolic immunity Base camp Co-immunity co-immunity symbolic immunity vertical tension base camp anthrotechnics\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-08-philosophize-改变人生不是换信念-而是移动你的大本营/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e87b1cc15318e66e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">35:25</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Philosophize This!</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0808</span></div>\n<h2>Changing your life isn&#x27;t swapping beliefs — it&#x27;s moving out of the casino you built</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">You are not an independent subject standing opposite the world; you are a product co-constituted by environment, body and a symbolic immune system. To change your life you have to change belief, action and world at the same time, rather than forcing yourself to &quot;think it through&quot;.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Sloterdijk</span><span class=\"tag\">Environmental shaping</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Dismounted Knights and a Hidden Ambush: How Henry I Captured His Own Brother Henry I made his knights fight on foot, then sprang a concealed fourth division against the enemy flank, taking his brother Robert prisoner and ending the fight for Normandy. The British History Podcast British History Podcast 505 – The Battle of Tinchebray Medieval history Normandy England War decisions Source criticism Orderic Vitalis Henry of Huntingdon siege castle\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-britishhist-亨利一世在坦什布赖靠下马步战加伏兵生擒长兄/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fdf8df11ad3c0078-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">48:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The British History Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>Dismounted Knights and a Hidden Ambush: How Henry I Captured His Own Brother</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Henry I made his knights fight on foot, then sprang a concealed fourth division against the enemy flank, taking his brother Robert prisoner and ending the fight for Normandy.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Medieval history</span><span class=\"tag\">Normandy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"DNA Repair Is the Key to Long Life: A Bowhead Whale Repair Protein Is Already in Human Trials New work finds that nearly half of hospitalized COVID-19 patients had dormant viruses reactivate in their bodies, with higher inflammation and cell damage a year later; meanwhile what long-lived animals like the bowhead whale share is not tumor-suppressor genes but stronger DNA repair, and drugs built on that idea have reached human trials. Nature Podcast Nature Podcast Briefing Chat: Is DNA repair the secret to a long life? Whales and mole-rats offer tantalizing hints Long COVID Dormant viruses DNA repair Anti-aging Human trials Bowhead whales dormant virus double-strand break CIRBP (cold-inducible RNA-binding protein) Sirt6 Fucoidan NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-naturepod-长新冠或源于休眠病毒-dna修复或为长寿关键/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/15a972da91ae970c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">13:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Nature Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>DNA Repair Is the Key to Long Life: A Bowhead Whale Repair Protein Is Already in Human Trials</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">New work finds that nearly half of hospitalized COVID-19 patients had dormant viruses reactivate in their bodies, with higher inflammation and cell damage a year later; meanwhile what long-lived animals like the bowhead whale share is not tumor-suppressor genes but stronger DNA repair, and drugs built on that idea have reached human trials.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Long COVID</span><span class=\"tag\">Dormant viruses</span>\n</div></div></a>"]